On Sat, 10 Oct 2026 13:54:49 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> Let's focus on doing this deterministically first. That's the core
> concept of this repo: deterministic entrypoints, with determinstic
> behavior as much as possible. Delegate to inference when needed, and let
> the model use the deterministic tools.

Agreed. Both patches below are deterministic, with no inference
anywhere. A patch edited before it lands is still missed, and I've
left that for later.

> What I'd like to do for this functionality is review the open patches on
> the list. We can then calculate their change IDs, and look for a commit
> with the same change ID.

I checked this again on today's trunk, and the change id doesn't
survive. "[PATCH v3] mail: Add just am to apply patches in any
workspace" [1] carries change id mylnzmrtzvww, but the commit it
became has yrowpvsnsvxz. git am never writes jj's change-id header,
so jj assigns a new one.

git patch-id --stable does the job the change id would have. It is
computed from a patch's diff, and the commit the patch became gets
the same id, even after a reworded message or a rebase. So it's the
same lookup you describe, just with a different id.

> Here's a question... if we apply the commit, will it apply as empty
> because it's already been applied before? So we could just apply open
> patches to a temporary branch, and if it's empty then it's considered
> applied?

I tried it with that same patch on trunk. Plain git am fails with
"Patch failed at 0001". git am -3 does say "No changes -- Patch
already applied." But that only works while no later commit has
touched the same lines. After that it conflicts, and you can't tell
that from a patch that was never applied. It also needs a worktree
for every patch. Patch-id compares each patch directly against the
commit that took it, so anything that landed later doesn't matter.

> I'm not quite sure how to handle it. What I want to avoid is focusing
> on only the N most recent commits, and thus potentially missing
> things. In theory we are doing a graph query - we want to see if the
> change represented by a patch is in ::trunk.

That's what it does now. It hashes every commit in ::trunk, with no
range: 1203 commits in 0.17s. Then it looks up every patch on the
list in that set.

> Also I think to do this more deterministically, we want to use a
> header. So if a message reports a patch as being closed, it includes a
> header referencing the patch message ID. Then scripts can look for those
> headers and add the committed tag to those patches.

Done. Each report is a reply to all on the patch, and it carries

    Committed: <message-id of the patch>

with the commit's hash and subject in the body. On the next run, a
patch already named in a Committed header is skipped, so running it
twice doesn't post anything twice.

mail-tag.sh now tags committed every message that a Committed header
names. It recomputes the tag on each run, like replied, so the tag
goes away if the report does. That's "[PATCH] notmuch: Tag patches
reported committed" [2]. It's on trunk because the hook lives in
dotfiles.

> This task should go in the mail branch, and only moderator will run it
> for now.

It's "[PATCH] mail: Add just committed to report patches trunk took"
[3], against mail@upstream. From the mail workspace:

    just committed          # checks ::trunk
    just commit-mail        # one commit per report

The reports come from your notmuch identity. I tested it on a scratch
copy of the list with its own notmuch database, so nothing went to
the real list. It reported the four patches trunk took unchanged, and
a second run reported nothing.

This replaces "[PATCH] mail: Add applied.sh to find patches trunk
applied" [4], which only searched a range and printed its results.
Please drop that one.

